<section xml:id="04-review-questions">
  <title>Review Questions</title>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-1">
    <statement>
      <p>The diagram below shows the graph of three circles, P, Q and R.</p>

      <p>Use it to answer the questions<term>.</term></p>

      <!-- div center -->

      <image source="figures/rq4-1.jpg" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>image</shortdescription>
      </image>

      <!-- end div center -->

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>State the coordinates of the centre of P, Q and R.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Find the radii of circles P, Q and R</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Write the equation of circle R in general form</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Write the equation of circle P in the standard form</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Write the equation of circle Q in the standard form</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the perimeter of the triangle formed by the centres of
              the three circles, correct to three significant figures.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>P(-2, 0), Q(1, 2), R(2.5, -2.5)</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>P <m>= 3</m> units, Q <m>= 2</m>units, R <m>= 2.5</m> units</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>4x^2 + 4y^2 - 20x + 20y + 25 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>(x + 2)^2 + y^2 = 3^2</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>(x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 2^2</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>13.5</m> units.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the general form of the circle equation with the following
        properties.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Centre <m>(1, -1)</m> and radius 7</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Centre <m>(-2, 1)</m> and radius 5</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Centre <m>(0, -3)</m> and radius 2</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Centre <m>(-3, -4)</m> and radius 8</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 2x + 2y - 47 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 4x - 2y - 20 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 6y + 5 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 6x + 8y - 39 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-3">
    <statement>
      <p>The diagram shows the graph of circles A, B and C.</p>

      <p>Use them to answer the following questions.</p>

      <!-- div center -->

      <image source="figures/rq4-4.jpg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>image</shortdescription>
      </image>

      <!-- end div center -->

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Write the equation of each circle in the general form</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the perimeter of the triangle formed by the centres of
              the circle. Leave your answer in surd form.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Circle A: <m>x^2 + y^2 + 4x - 4y + 4 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Circle B: <m>x^2 + y^2 - 4x - 5 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Circle C: <m>x^2 + y^2 + 4x - 4y + 4 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>10 + 4\sqrt{5}</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A TV remote has a range of <m>8</m>m in all directions. If the TV is
        <m>5</m>m East and <m>3</m>m South, write an equation to represent
        the area within which the TV receives signals from the remote. Can
        the TV receive signals from the remote if the remote is located
        <m>2</m>m West? Justify your response.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 10x + 6y - 30 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Yes, since the distance between the position of the remote,
              <m>(-2, 0)</m>, and the position of the TV is less than the
              range of the remote.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        An Uber driver operates within <m>30</m>km of a post office at all
        angles. His house is <m>5</m>km West and <m>12</m>km south of a post
        office.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write an equation to represent the driver's travel boundary
              from the post office using the driver's house as the origin.
              Leave your equation in standard form.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the furthest distance between the driver's house and his
              travel boundary.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the shortest distance between the driver's house and the
              driver's travel boundary.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>(x + 5)^2 + (y + 12)^2 = 30^2</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>43</m> km</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>17</m> km</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-6">
    <statement>
      <p>Write the general equation of a circle with:</p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Centre <m>(4, -1)</m> and radius <m>\sqrt{11}</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Centre <m>(-2, -3)</m> and radius <m>2\frac{1}{2}</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Centre <m>(0, -3)</m> and radius <m>\frac{1}{3}</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Centre <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}, -1\right)</m> and radius
              <m>1\frac{1}{2}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 8x + 2y + 6 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>4x^2 + 4y^2 + 16x + 24y + 27 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>9x^2 + 9y^2 + 54y + 80 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - x + 2y - 1 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write each of the following circles in the standard form. Also,
        state the centre and radius of each circle.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 2x + 2y - 47 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 6y = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 12x - 64 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 4x - 8y + 4 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>2x^2 + 2y^2 - 2x - 2y - 7 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>3x^2 + 3y^2 + 12x - 2y + 4 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(x - 1)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 7^2</m>, Centre <m>= (1, -1)</m>and
              radius <m>= 7</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>x^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 3^2</m>, Centre <m>= (0, -3)</m> and radius
              <m>= 3</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(x - 6)^2 + y^2 = 10^2</m>, Centre <m>= (6, 0)</m> and radius
              <m>= 10</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(x + 2)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 4^2</m>, Centre <m>= (-2, 4)</m> and
              radius <m>= 4</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\left(x - \frac{1}{2}\right)^2 + \left(y -
              \frac{1}{2}\right)^2 = 2^2</m>, Centre <m>= \left(\frac{1}{2},
              \frac{1}{2}\right)</m> and radius <m>= 2</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\left(x + 2\right)^2 + \left(y - \frac{1}{3}\right)^2 =
              \left(\frac{5}{3}\right)^2</m>, Centre <m>= \left(-2,
              \frac{1}{3}\right)</m> and radius <m>= \frac{5}{3} =
              1\frac{2}{3}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-8">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The endpoints of the diameter of a circle are <m>(0, 5)</m> and
        <m>(0, -7)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>Find the</p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>radius and centre of the circle</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>general equation of the circle.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Radius <m>= 6</m> units, Centre <m>= (0, -1)</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 2y - 35 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-9">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The endpoints of the diameter of a circle are <m>(6, 0)</m> and
        <m>(-2, 0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>Find the</p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>radius and centre of the circle</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>general equation of the circle.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Radius <m>= 4</m> units, Centre <m>= (2, 0)</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 4x - 12 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-10">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The endpoints of the diameter of a circle are <m>(-4, 3)</m> and
        <m>(4, -3)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>Find the</p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>radius and centre of the circle</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>general equation of the circle.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Radius <m>= 5</m> units, Centre <m>= (0, 0)</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 25 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-11">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In each of the following questions, coordinates of three points are
        provided. Write the general equation of the circle that satisfies
        all three points. State the centre and radius of the resulting
        circle.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>(-5, 0)</m>, <m>(-1, -4)</m> and <m>(-1, 4)</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>(6, -1)</m>, <m>(3, -4)</m> and <m>(0, -1)</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>x^2 + y^2 + 2x - 15 = 0</m>, Centre <m>(-1, 0)</m>, Radius
              <m>= 4</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>x^2 + y^2 - 6x + 2y + 1 = 0</m>, Centre <m>= (3, -1)</m>,
              Radius <m>= 3</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-12">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equations of the line tangent and normal to the circle.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 100</m> at point <m>(-8, -6)</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 = 49</m> at point <m>(7, 0)</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 24 = 0</m> at <m>(1, -5)</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Tangent equation is <m>3y + 4x + 50 = 0</m>; Normal equation is
              <m>4y - 3x = 0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Tangent equation is <m>x - 7 = 0</m>; Normal equation is <m>y =
              0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Tangent equation is <m>y + 5 = 0</m>; Normal equation is <m>x -
              1 = 0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-13">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the length of the tangent from the point:</p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(-8, 6)</m> to the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 - 25 = 0</m>, leave
              your answer in surd form
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>(3, 6)</m> to the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 - 4y - 5 = 0</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(7, -3)</m> to the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 + 10x - 4y + 13 =
              0</m>, leave your answer in surd form
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\left(5, -\frac{1}{2}\right)</m> to the circle <m>4x^2 +
              4y^2 + 12x + 4y - 15 = 0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>5\sqrt{3}</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>4</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>3\sqrt{17}</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>6</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-14">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The diagram below shows a graph of a circle. Line BA is a tangent to
        the circle at B.
      </p>

      <!-- div center -->

      <image source="figures/rq4-19.jpg" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>image</shortdescription>
      </image>

      <!-- end div center -->

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Find the equation of the circle.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Find <m>|</m>AB<m>|</m>, leave your answer in surds</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\left(x + 5\right)^2 + \left(y + \frac{1}{2}\right)^2 =
              \left(\frac{5}{2}\right)^2</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>4\sqrt{2}</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-15">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the equation of all points equidistant from <m>A(-4,
        0)</m> and <m>B(2, 8)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p><m>4y + 3x - 13 = 0</m></p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-16">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equation of the locus of points which moves so that its
        distance from the lines <m>3y - x = 0</m> and <m>3x - y = 0</m> is
        the same.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p><m>y - x = 0</m></p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-17">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the locus of point <m>R(x, y)</m> that is always 2 units from
        the line <m>x + 5 = 0</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p><m>x + 7 = 0</m> or <m>x + 3 = 0</m></p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-18">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If P(-2, 1) and Q(4, -1) are two fixed points and A(<m>x</m>,
        <m>y</m>) moves such that <m>|</m>AP<m>|</m>:<m>|</m>AQ<m>| =
        2</m>:1. Find the equation of the locus<term>.</term>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p><m>3x^2 + 3y^2 - 36x + 10y + 63 = 0</m></p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="04-review-questions-19">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A point A(<m>x</m>, <m>y</m>) moves so that AC and AD are
        perpendicular. <m>C(-2, 0)</m> and <m>D(6, 4)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>Find the equation of the locus A.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p><m>x^2 + y^2 - 4x - 4y - 12 = 0</m></p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
</section>
